I've played this for nearly fifty hours, so let me tell you this upfront: this game is all about looking for the next best weapon.
As one should expect, you'll start with ♥♥♥♥♥♥ weapons (low damage, low accuracy, low capacity), and that makes the game very boring.
Pros:
- The art is great;
- The voices are great;
- The non-playable characters are interesting (except for the main villain, for which I really couldn't care about);
Cons:
- You'll spend 50% of your time wandering in the wasteland, doing nothing but going from point A to point B, because every mission objective is so unecessarily far apart from the others, and difficult to access;
- 10% of the time you'll spend looking around for boxes, lockers and chests to open, because better weapons, money and ammo are inside them (you can buy ammo in vending machines, but it won't be enough for a mission, until you make enough cash to buy more inventory space)
- 10% will be spent opening those boxes with a quick button press, and a long button press to pick the stuff that were inside them (this is so stupid);
- 20% of the time you will be comparing guns, shields and grenades in order to decide which ones to keep and which ones to sell, using one of the most cumbersome interfaces I've ever seen for doing it. The commercials praise the fact that Borderlands 2 has a GAZILLION WEAPONS, as if 99% of them weren't completely useless and just a waste of time;
- All that ♥♥♥♥♥♥♥t leaves the remaining 10% of your gaming time for doing what is the reason you bought this game in the first place: shooting people and aliens;
More cons:
- The game is very repetitive, I mean, enormously;
- Most jokes aren't clever at all;
- Everytime you open the game, it spends half a minute at the title screen "looking for DLC" and there's nothing you can do to skip it;
- When played on co-op, the loot is not equally distributed between players. While you're busy shooting bad guys and helping the team, one team mate will do nothing but scavenge the loot and pick all the good stuff for himself, leaving the rest of the team with nothing;
- Most missions are the "go to place X which is far as ♥♥♥♥ from where you are right now, pick up all the Y items which are scattered around a very large area as well, bring them back, and also kill those guys" type. Rinse, repeat;
Game of the Year? Villain of the Year? Really? What is wrong with gaming these days!?

Undertale is a successfully Kickstarted game made mostly by a single person – Toby Fox. To specify the game’s genre, think of something like “creative bullet hell and comedy adventure with focus on decision making and emotional story“.
You might have already read or heard a lot of praise about Undertale, but I’ll rather start at the supposedly weakest point of the game, the graphics. Let’s be honest, they look poor even in compare to other pixelart game graphics. But as you’ll play more, you’ll realise they perfectly do their job, allowing all surprising changes and weirdness, that you’ll meet through the game, to shine.
In many other games, once you get to some sort of new gameplay feature or mini-game (
), you’ll get to deal with them (or use them) repeatedly until they make you feel bored, for getting overused. (That also lowers your entertainment, making you more probably to rush through the specific game’s content.) Undertale is absolutely different, showing the developer’s hard work towards constant surprises for the player, with various gameplay features programmed just for specific scenes. Besides that fact,
NPC has a unique way of attacking; at least every type – but until you won’t want to kill everybody, you’ll mostly not meet each regular NPC more than twice.
I’ve seen few people play Undertale and I have to say that it’s not a game for everybody to like. I’ve seen people who just took this as another JRPG, rushing through the content, fighting just with everybody and talking with disappointment like “So, what’s so great about that?” afterwards. The main point is, the game favour people who want to think like “Wait, do I have to kill him/her/it?” with a reward of a lot of entertainment and fun. People who tend to think with more creativity will love this game. Even though the battle options are straightforward, there are more possible outcomes of fights besides killing the enemy. You
don’t have to fight, you can find a way to turn your enemies into friends.
To summarize the story without spoilers – Undertale is about monsters that have lost a war against humans on Earth. Monsters that survived ran deep into a cavern, where they got locked by a human magic. They somehow survived thanks to magic and technology, and thanks to their hope for better future; hope to see the Sun again.
Mt. Ebott is a weird place – people who had gone there went missing. And you take role of a kid who falls into the mountain, into the kingdom of monsters. And from this moment, everything else is under your control. It’s up to your decision what will happen with everybody. The game has, besides some small differences, three big endings, each with absolutely different outcome.
I’m not a type of person who would usually replay games (
) but after finishing Undertale for the first time in about 6 hours, I had to take another try for different ending... and wow. You’ll eventually find a lot more information about the characters and monsters’ history. You’ll also realise many things that together shows you the depth of the story. What may surprise you is also the fact that the game knows pretty well if you are replaying the game for another time, characters have slightly different dialogues... but I’ll not spoil more of it, let’s just say that it’s totally expected from you to play Undertale multiple times for complete experience.
While Undertale really has an emotional story, as many reviews full of tears may tell you, don’t forget that sadness is not the only emotion. The game will also try to stimulate your happiness – not just in a way of making yourself happy for not killing your favourite character, but also by humour. There’s a joke, gag or a pop culture reference (
) around every corner, probably even in every character. You’ll see.
The final thing that gets the praise is the Toby Fox’s iconic soundtrack. Undertale has a perfect soundtrack, mostly composited from chiptunes. There are so many great melodies that will get stuck into your head and you’ll love them. An interesting fact, most of the game music was made before designing and programming of specific scenes they was made for – Toby Fox said that it helped him to decide how the scene should go. (
)
Undertale is something so unique and unexpectedly awesome, that it deserves praise without objection. It’ll make you laugh, cry, or just keeps you entertained from 6 to 25 hours (depends on if you decide to replay the game for different endings;
).
